- [2022 annual real wages fell 0.9 percent](https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/kaiken/daijin/0000194708_00524.html) - Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, 2023-02-07, reporting on 2022 annual results. The ministry said real wages were down 0.9 percent in 2022 because nominal wages rose 2.1 percent but CPI rose 3.0 percent, and it explicitly linked the policy response to continued wage increases and a 1 trillion yen human-investment package. This is one of the clearest outcome items. Temporal fit: 2022 annual outcome reported just after period end. Ruler attribution: largely outcome under Kishida, though wage-setting is partly private-sector and inflation external. Contrary points: a negative real-wage result weakens the claim of strong 2022 prosperity delivery. Lenses: 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"加藤大臣会見概要 ｜令和５年２月７日｜大臣記者会見","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare","publication_date":"2023-02-07","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/kaiken/daijin/0000194708_00524.html","claim":"The ministry said real wages fell 0.9 percent in 2022 because nominal wages rose 2.1 percent while the consumer-price index rose 3.0 percent, and it linked the policy response to continued wage increases and a 1 trillion yen human-investment package.","locator":"Reporter Q&A and minister answer in the open page","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry statement reporting the annual outcome and policy response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 annual outcome reported in February 2023","ruler_attribution":"Outcome under Kishida's term, but only partly under his direct control because prices and wage bargaining were external and decentralized.","contrary_evidence":["Real wages falling is a direct negative on household prosperity, even if policy was trying to reverse it."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents and kept 13 distinct URLs. The main families were official PMO/Kantei records, IMF surveillance, MHLW labor statistics, and Reuters reporting.

Rejected or treated as context only:
- Duplicate Kantei pages repeating the same policy speech in archive/index form.
- Later 2023-2026 Kishida economy pages that went beyond the 2022 window and were not needed once 2022 sources were in hand.
- Broad BOJ policy statements: useful for monetary backdrop, but not directly Kishida-controlled, so I did not elevate them above context.
- General OECD background pieces that discussed Japan’s labor market or skills but did not add Kishida-specific 2022 choices beyond what the Kantei speeches already showed.
- Purely descriptive stats pages with no ruler-specific decision content.
- The local 2022 economy facts from WDI/Maddison/UNDP were not re-fetched, per your instruction, and were used only as the structured prior baseline.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have strong direct evidence on 5B.2, the quality of Kishida’s economic appointments, beyond his use of councils, experts, and government-wide coordination.
- 5B.5 remains only weakly evidenced. I have fairness-oriented tax design and SME exemptions, but not a clean anti-corruption or anti-capture item tied to economic policy.
- The real-wage and inflation picture is clearly negative in 2022, but attribution is mixed because price shocks were external and monetary policy sat mainly with the BOJ.
- I have much better evidence on policy intent and macro stabilization than on durable distributional outcomes or productivity gains by year-end.
- More search would help on firm-level investment, household income distribution, productivity and labor-force participation in 2022, but it is unlikely to change the broad conclusion that Kishida’s 2022 economic record was active and interventionist, yet still constrained by inflation, weak real wages, and external shocks.

